think The running thread is the Season G scene-read floor. The prior lap built image/cluster_minimum_spanning_tree.rye (the skeleton, the MST bridge edges), which reads mst_weight (sum of edges) and mst_bottleneck (largest edge). The REMEMBER NEXT line named a cluster-tree DIAMETER read as one crux-first, agent-doable option -- the longest walk across the skeleton, a whole-tree number no single edge holds. Chose it: Lindy-first (a relational tree primitive read for years), crux-first (the hardest-solvable move that opens the rest), no fetch/license/key gate.
think Read the MST module and witness to compose cleanly. Built image/cluster_tree_diameter.rye: tree_diameter (all-pairs longest path via a bounded breadth-first walk over the tree's own edges, one walk per cluster), diameter_endpoints (the two poles, lowest pair on a tie for determinism), tree_eccentricity (a cluster's farthest reach), is_diameter_endpoint (the pole test). Composed over cluster_mst.build_mst / mst_weight / mst_bottleneck, cross-checked never re-derived.
think Designed the crux as two shapes over the MST's own two scenes. THREE-CLUSTER STAR (edges 1-2 gap 2, 1-3 gap 3): longest path 2->1->3 = 5 spends both edges, so diameter EQUALS the whole weight 5 (a two-edge tree is a single path) yet EXCEEDS the bottleneck 3; poles the leaves, hub the center of least eccentricity 3. FOUR-CLUSTER STAR (edges 1-2, 1-3 gap 3, 1-4 gap 9): longest path 2->1->4 = 12 spends the 9-arm and one 3-arm while the third arm branches off unwalked, so diameter sits STRICTLY BETWEEN bottleneck and weight, 9 < 12 < 15 -- three distinct numbers; far outlier a pole, hub still the center. The decisive lesson: the diameter is a THIRD tree invariant, coinciding with the weight only when the tree is a single path.
